        {
          "path": "_m/g/c/7/x/temple-bar-dublin-wp/earthcam-cultural-nigh.jpg",
          "filename": "EarthCam in Temple Bar, Dublin.jpg",
          "caption": "The EarthCam in the cultural, nightlife, and tourist quarter of Temple Bar in Dublin, Ireland. The camera is mounted at 21 Essex Street East, Dublin 2, D02 T209. It points towards the Temple Bar Pub, situated at a crossroads, and at the constant pedestrian traffic that flows past. Locals and foreign visitors pose in the street and wave to distant friends viewing over the internet, while speaking to them on their mobile phones. There is also a microphone associated with the camera which sends the street sounds of Dublin live all over the world including people's voices, footsteps, the sound of wheelie luggage clattering across street cobblestones, the metallic rattle of chains when cyclists lock their bikes to poles, occasional car engines and horns, and the cries of seagulls, a distinctly Dublin sound that would sour milk, so evocative it touches the hearts of far-flung emigrants from the city. The shouts of drinkers are louder at night when they leave the bars. If James Joyce had a web browser in Trieste, this webcam would have wrecked his productivity.",
          "alt": "The EarthCam in the cultural, nightlife, and tourist quarter of Temple Bar in Dublin, Ireland. The camera is mounted at 21 Essex Street East, Dublin 2, D02 T209. It points towards the Temple Bar Pub, situated at a crossroads, and at the constant pedestrian traffic that flows past. Locals and foreign visitors pose in the street and wave to distant friends viewing over the internet, while speaking to them on their mobile phones. There is also a microphone associated with the camera which sends the street sounds of Dublin live all over the world including people's voices, footsteps, the sound of wheelie luggage clattering across street cobblestones, the metallic rattle of chains when cyclists lock their bikes to poles, occasional car engines and horns, and the cries of seagulls, a distinctly Dublin sound that would sour milk, so evocative it touches the hearts of far-flung emigrants from the city. The shouts of drinkers are louder at night when they leave the bars. If James Joyce had a web browser in Trieste, this webcam would have wrecked his productivity.",
          "width": 2048,
          "height": 1536,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
          "artist": "Spideog",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EarthCam_in_Temple_Bar,_Dublin.jpg",
